A few thoughts about spinlocks that I found out based on live first hand performance problems:

==========================================================================================================================
<a> The myth of using a spin  lock around a very small critical section (perhaps two dword writes + 1 dword read?)

Because the total time spent in the critical section involves lock commit times which can increase with #cores/#processors,
also dont forget the CAS times, these are bracket costs you pay.

Next, dont forget you could get preempted anytime before you release the lock no matter how small your CS.

Consider the delay above to be `x' cycles, during which more contenders could start coming up. In the hot lock case this
can get worse on extremely multi core systems. 

Ok now you have say 30 contenders on the spinlock, the spin time for the 30th thread is nearly 30x. 

This excessive spinning means these threads are using up their entire timeslices on tbeir respective cores so its quite possible
they are starving the lock holder from releasing the lock though thats unlikely in high #core systems.
==========================================================================================================================

==========================================================================================================================
<b> Should I use fair spinlocks or unfair ones?

UNFAIR SPINLOCKS:
Ok this means when the #contenders keeps on increasing its quite possible some lock is starved entirely. Now the problem
I faced was that I was implementing sleep locks using spinlocks around the turnstile queue, while sleepLock and sleepUnlock
this turnstile spin lock is contended.  What I noticed was that the sleepUnlock thread was being starved because more and
more threads kept on hogging the turnstile spinlock. This is definitely a place where fifo spinlocks woudl have helped
aka guaranteed no starvation though in this case we also need explicit priorities in the spinlock perhaps a two level 
priority because being able to release the sleepLock helps the system make better progress and you want this to happen
instantaneously not ordered behind 20 other locks in fifo order.

FAIR SPINLOCKS:
Not as rosey as it may sound. If you spin without backoff (where backoff means selective sleep could be sleep with 
increasing quantum bounded at the top) then the  threads could get scheduled in the order that does not agree with the
lock's fifo order. THIS IS THE PATHOLOGICAL CASE FOR FIFO ORDERED SPINLOCKS, fair sleeplocks dont face this problem since
they are not contending in the scheduler's runqueue.
==========================================================================================================================


==========================================================================================================================
<c> To backoff or not?

My conclusion based on practical runs being all spinlocks (unless you are in the kernel and at an exalted ISR level) should
have backoff. Backoff can be of two types:

-> Hey I just spun `n' times and didnt get the lock sleep then come back and spin `n' more times
-> Hey the #lockcontenders in the fifo lock is 200 theres no point in spinning  just backoff and come back later
   < Btw this can also be used by unfair locsk >
